 &lt;td&gt;See the tutorial&lt;/td&gt;
 &lt;/tr&gt;
 &lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;</description></item><item><title>Workspaces</title><link>/pr-preview/pr-1339/cloud/concepts/workspaces/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/pr-preview/pr-1339/cloud/concepts/workspaces/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;A workspace is a named grouping inside an organization that brings together the teams who do a piece of work, the environments they deploy to, and the designs and views they work on. Workspaces exist so an administrator can draw a boundary around a project or a stage of a pipeline, such as staging or production, and decide in one place which teams reach which infrastructure connections.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A workspace does two separate jobs, and keeping them apart is the key to the model:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Environments</title><link>/pr-preview/pr-1339/cloud/concepts/environments/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/pr-preview/pr-1339/cloud/concepts/environments/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;An environment is a named group of connections, such as Kubernetes clusters, together with the credentials those connections use. Environments matter for two reasons:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;An environment is a deploy target.&lt;/strong&gt; When you deploy a design, you choose the environment to deploy it to, and the design deploys to that environment&amp;rsquo;s connections.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;An environment is how a workspace exposes connections.&lt;/strong&gt; A workspace never holds connections directly. It holds environments, and teams assigned to the workspace reach the connections inside them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Group connections the way you deploy: one environment per pipeline stage, region, or purpose, so that choosing a target means choosing one environment instead of a list of clusters.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>